(2517746, 341816, 39007, '<td valign="top">Cute idea, I was interested to see the spelling errors were deliberate, nice attention to detail!<br/> <br/>There is one thing I have not seen in the comments below that could help you earn a better score next time. The image looks pixellated or blocky. This is because it has been compressed too much, the image is only 59kb. You are allowed to use upto 150kb, its best to use most or all of this. In Photoshop use "save for web" there is a little triangle next to the preset dropdown (top right) - click on it. Select optimise to file size - type in 150 - click done! <br/> <br/>Most other software doesn''t have this, try saving at 9 out of 10 quality, check the filesize and resave with a lower quality if needed.<br/> <br/>I''m sure doing this and turning the date stamp off will help you score higher. The date is encoded in the picture file anyway so most people leave the date stamp off permanently, you can check the date by looking at image properties.<br/> <br/>Good luck!</td>', 'Cute idea, I was interested to see the spelling errors were deliberate, nice attention to detail! There is one thing I have not seen in the comments below that could help you earn a better score next time. The image looks pixellated or blocky. This is because it has been compressed too much, the image is only 59kb. You are allowed to use upto 150kb, its best to use most or all of this. In Photoshop use "save for web" there is a little triangle next to the preset dropdown (top right) - click on it. Select optimise to file size - type in 150 - click done!  Most other software doesn''t have this, try saving at 9 out of 10 quality, check the filesize and resave with a lower quality if needed. I''m sure doing this and turning the date stamp off will help you score higher. The date is encoded in the picture file anyway so most people leave the date stamp off permanently, you can check the date by looking at image properties. Good luck!', '2006-06-14 19:01:33.000000', NULL, 0),
